00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.890
'cause everything is just achy and sore.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:04.080
If I have three glasses of alcohol,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.381
all of sudden I can't
function the next day.

00:03:06.381 --> 00:03:07.620
(sigh)

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.453
Sigh.

00:03:08.453 --> 00:03:10.372
- Hi, it's Babs, and
here's a quick health tip.

00:03:10.372 --> 00:03:12.400
Tablespoon of apple cider vinegar,

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.233
Bragg's,

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:16.230
in a cup of room temperature water,

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.870
taken right before bedtime,
help with weight loss,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.020
help with your gut,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.810
unbelievable,

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:22.980
it gives you a good night sleep.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:24.690
- Dude, she wrote everything

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.490
that's troubling society right now:

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.750
weight loss, gut health,
digestion, complexion,

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:29.583
so much more.

00:03:29.583 --> 00:03:32.712
Imagine I spent 10 years educating myself

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:36.300
on the pathophysiology,
and the pharmacology,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.240
and the anatomy,

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.640
when all I had to go was, floop!

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.480
And now I just solved all the problems

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:42.640
that are ailing society.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.240
Why don't we just give this
to everybody and end this?

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.820
- You're not gonna believe
what just happened.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:47.670
- Promise, I am.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.750
- I go to get my flu shot.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.870
- Don't do me wrong here.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:51.703
- And she goes,

00:03:51.703 --> 00:03:53.100
"All right, well do you want
it in your left or right arm?"

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.010
I said, "My left, but I
have a long sleeve shirt on,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.200
so I don't know if it's
gonna pull up all the way.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.320
I might just have to take
my arm out, if that's okay."

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.410
And before I could even do that,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.130
she doesn't even answer me.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:05.963
Really.

00:04:05.963 --> 00:04:06.796
She doesn't give me an option.

00:04:06.796 --> 00:04:07.761
She goes, "Oh, that's okay.

00:04:07.761 --> 00:04:08.594
This happens all the time.

00:04:08.594 --> 00:04:09.520
No issues."

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:10.570
Bop!

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:14.750
Through, she gives me the
shot through my shirt.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.140
So now do I have a piece of cotton

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:21.360
in my bloodstream just floating around?

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.470
- No, this is not normal

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.600
and you shouldn't be doing this.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.970
One, you have to look at
where you're injecting.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.370
You have to see a clean surface.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:28.280
You have to clean it

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.100
with a alcohol wipe in
order to disinfect the area.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.200
Also, you wanna make
sure you're not injecting

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.440
into a mole or already infected source.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.590
Imagine underneath the shirt,

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:40.700
she had a big cellulitis and
infection there, an abscess,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.600
and now you're injecting into it.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.290
You gotta visualize where
you're injecting, number one.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:46.460
Number two, to answer your question,

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.330
you don't just have cotton floating around

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.670
in your body or whatever material

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.660
because likely the needle
penetrated the holes

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.740
of the fibers and didn't
cut through the fiber.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:55.770
And, even if it did,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.300
it wouldn't just be floating in your body.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.950
It would just be in that area,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.850
and then your body would
naturally push it out.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.360
While I don't think that's
the biggest problem.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.600
I do think that's an irresponsible
way to give a flu shot.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.580
If that really happened,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.590
I would probably complain about it

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:08.490
'cause that's not the norm.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.080
- There are 20 case
reports in the literature

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.990
of people whose colons
have either exploded

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.920
or caught fire during surgery.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.490
The colon makes methane
and some hydrogen gas

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:19.630
and if that combines with
oxygen and a source of energy,

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:23.140
like a Bovie or a laser, that can ignite.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:24.960
- A Bovie is the electrocautery tool

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.100
that we sometimes use instead
of a scalpel to cut tissue.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.830
Benefits of that is it actually

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:31.730
cuts and cauterizes at the same time,

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:32.850
so it minimizes bleeding.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.491
- There's been a case where
the surgeon caught fire

00:05:35.491 --> 00:05:37.970
and another couple cases
where the colon exploded

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:38.830
and the patient died.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.300
- Logically, it all makes
sense and it could happen.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.890
I'll just put it this way.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.630
When I counsel my patients,
when they're going

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.210
for pre-op screening

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.490
and I counsel them on the
procedure they're gonna get,

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.040
I don't usually include

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.769
exploding colon as part of the risks.

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:54.130
(explosion)

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:56.160
- How can your body replicate the feeling

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.160
of falling from high
altitude in a nightmare,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.573
if you've never fallen like that.

00:06:00.573 --> 00:06:02.750
- This is actually a really
good and interesting question.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:03.750
So psychologists believe

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:06.670
that the fear of falling
is actually an evolutionary

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:08.370
or innate trait within us

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:09.700
to actually protect us.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.530
So it's different from
the fear of heights,

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:12.660
which some people have,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:13.960
but almost every human

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.920
and almost every animal
has the fear of falling.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.840
They've even tested this in infants

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.820
as young as like six months old.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.000
If they put them near a glass floor

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.000
that's a few feet up

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.067
and then their mothers call them

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:26.320
the infants will actually
avoid the glass floor

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.153
or not come at all

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:28.540
because, even at that age,

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:29.860
without the experience of falling,

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:32.240
they innately know that
it's a dangerous thing.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:34.725
- ASAP Science comes through
again with some accurate info!

00:06:34.725 --> 00:06:35.558
(claps)

00:06:35.558 --> 00:06:36.391
Well done!

00:06:36.391 --> 00:06:37.300
Something I can add to this conversation,

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.310
because I work in a neonatology ward

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.760
as a family medicine doctor,

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.750
one of the reflexes we test in babies

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.330
is called the moro reflex.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.320
It's kind of evil when you see us do it,

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:47.195
when we essentially

00:06:47.195 --> 00:06:48.970
rapidly dropped the baby.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:50.430
You're not actually making contact,

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.640
the baby's not falling,

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.450
but you basically lower
the baby really quickly.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.227
The baby kind of tries to
grasp on and save itself

00:06:56.227 --> 00:06:59.680
because it has the
innate survival mechanism

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.360
known as the moro reflex.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.100
So this is something we
are innately born with.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.047
Another species, this is very evident,

00:07:05.047 --> 00:07:06.240
is puppies.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.290
A newborn puppy, you put 'im near stair,

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:09.960
they know not to go down the stairs.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.190
They don't just throw themselves down

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.290
even though no one walked up to them

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:14.123
and was like,

00:07:14.123 --> 00:07:15.020
"Hey, these are stairs,

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.090
if you go down gravity'll go (spirts)."

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.750
Click here for me and a mortician

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.770
partnering up watching Six Feet Under

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.470
or check me out another day in the life

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:27.339
in my hospital on a COVID
ward or unit or wing

00:07:27.339 --> 00:07:28.820
whichever you like.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.133
As always stay happy and healthy.

00:07:31.133 --> 00:07:32.524
Click!

00:07:32.524 --> 00:07:36.357
(upbeat dance music fades in)

